= with a string literal operand does prefix matching
(diag.equals_vs_strict_equals, info tier, suggests ==), on either side.
"Logged" = "Log" is .T. while "Logged" != "Log" is also .T. (AGENTS.md
string equality gotcha).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE StatusIs;
	:PARAMETERS sStatus;
	:IF sStatus = "Log";
		:RETURN 1;
	:ENDIF;
	:IF "Done" = sStatus;
		:RETURN 2;
	:ENDIF;
	:RETURN 0;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE StatusIs;
	:PARAMETERS sStatus;
	:IF sStatus = "Log";
		:RETURN 1;
	:ENDIF;

	:IF "Done" = sStatus;
		:RETURN 2;
	:ENDIF;
	:RETURN 0;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
3:14-3:15 info equals_vs_strict_equals: For strings, '=' does prefix matching. Use '==' for exact string comparisons
6:13-6:14 info equals_vs_strict_equals: For strings, '=' does prefix matching. Use '==' for exact string comparisons
